{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And Esau was a man who knew hunting, a man of the field.</b> For he would hunt women away from under their husbands for all forty years, and according to our Rabbis of blessed memory (Bava Batra 16b) he forcibly violated a betrothed maiden. Therefore he called him 'who knew hunting,' that is, hunting women away from under their husbands — meaning those who sit under their husbands. But he would always go to hunt them in the fields, as it is said concerning the betrothed maiden: ״כִּי בַשָּׂדֶה מְצָאָהּ״ (Deuteronomy 22:27). Therefore he called Esau 'a man of the field,' for he would go to seek them there so that her voice would not be heard when she cries out. And 'hunting' would be in the literal sense of prey, or in the sense of hunting with his mouth by means of enticement, in the manner of all who entice." "<b>And Jacob was a wholehearted man.</b> For he was born circumcised according to our Rabbis of blessed memory (Avot de-Rabbi Natan, ch. 2); therefore he was fenced off from forbidden sexual relations and would dwell in tents and cleave to his wife and not to another, as it is written (Deuteronomy 5:27) ״שׁוּבוּ לָכֶם לְאָהֳלֵיכֶם״, which our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Shabbat 87a) concerning the permitting of conjugal relations."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The verse cited from Deuteronomy 22:27 is normalized from the Hebrew-only form given in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:08.552355778Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}